[?2004l[?2004hroot@nid004145:~# chmod +x /root/submission/run.sh
[?2004l[?2004hroot@nid004145:~# cat > /root/submission/README.md <<'MD'
[?2004l[?2004h> # Four-lepton Poisson counting analysis
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Run the reproducible analysis with:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h> /root/submission/run.sh INPUT_JSON OUTPUT_JSON
[?2004l[?2004h> ```
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The input JSON has `channels`, `observed`, `background`, and `signal` arrays. Each channel is treated as an independent Poisson count. The program reports total Poisson log likelihoods (including `log(k!)`) for background and SM signal-plus-background, and the fixed-hypothesis statistic `q=-2(logL_sb-logL_b)`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> For the p-value, one million background-only Poisson toy datasets are generated with a fixed NumPy seed. Smaller values of this q are more signal-like, so the reported one-sided p-value is the fraction with `q_toy <= q_observed`. The finite-toy estimate uses `(N_extreme+1)/(N_toys+1)`, and the Gaussian-equivalent significance is `Phi^-1(1-p)`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> For the measurement, the likelihood is profiled as a function of physical signal strength `mu >= 0`, with means `background + mu*signal`. Numerical optimization finds `mu_hat`; the one-standard-deviation endpoints are where `-2 Delta log L=1`. If the lower crossing would be below physical `mu=0`, the lower edge is reported as zero.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Diagnostics are always regenerated at `submission/diagnostics/test_statistic_toys.png` and `submission/diagnostics/mu_profile.png`.
[?2004l[?2004h> MD
[?2004l[?2004hroot@nid004145:~# /root/submission/run.sh /root/data/four_lepton_counts.json /root/results/results.json
[?2004l[?2004hroot@nid004145:~# cat /root/results/results.json && ls -lh /root/submission/diagnostics /root/submission
